        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <div><b>Objective:</b></div><div><b><br></b></div><div><div>NASA has a growing interest in using extended reality (XR)
technologies (virtual reality, augmented reality, and hybrid reality)
verification and validation activities for the Spaceflight Program. To meet
NASA minimum specifications, hardware systems are required to be verified human
rated. Analysis, inspection, demonstration, and testing are several methods by
which verification activities can be completed. Method determination depends on
the requirement type, form factors of products, and test facility availability
where verification activities can take place. Verification activities can be
costly, as developers must often build high-fidelity physical mockups for
certain verification tests. Leveraging XR technologies in human-in-the-loop
testing requires little to no hardware, construction costs, or storage
facilities, which is significant in cost savings. XR allows NASA systems to be
replicated by a high-fidelity virtual reality simulation which approximates the
appearance, size, and functionality of a real-world experience. <o:p></o:p></div><div><br></div>
<div>The validity of verification outcomes from XR methodologies
are currently unclear. The researchers for this investigation will communicate
directly with NASA subject matter experts (SMEs) on using XR for activities
that can help this study. The biggest concern is that verification data
collected with XR technology would not be comparable to data collected in a
physical mockup using traditional methods. This could result in additional risk
when used for flight readiness decisions. Taking this into consideration, NASA
currently limits use of XR technologies for developmental test activities only.
NASA needs data and theoretical knowledge to develop guidelines for how XR
technology can be applied without increasing human health and performance risk
for the future. This research will help NASA understand XR technologies
strengths, limitations, and gaps. <o:p></o:p></div><div><br></div>

<div>This project has the following aim:<o:p></o:p></div>

<div>1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; To gather
and synthesize available knowledge and generate new data that will help
stakeholders make informed decisions regarding the use of XR technologies for
verification-related activities.<o:p></o:p></div></div><div><b><br></b></div><div><b>Approach:</b></div><div>

<div>The literature review was carried out by the project team, an
overview was created of how XR technology was used for verification and related
activities, such as operational testing, examining evaluations, and training.
Gathered information on XR training was the start towards understanding the
strengths and weaknesses of XR for different tasks. The researchers interviewed
experts and stakeholders, that allowed the researchers an understanding on XR
technologies success. It also helped pinpoint the strengths, weaknesses, and
barriers to implementation, while giving lessons on verification. This study
included a pilot study to replicate verification activities using “equivalent”
virtual and physical mockups. Results of the virtual and physical mockups are
compared and provide validation for XR Verification Framework. The deliverables
of Phase I are used for a separate Phase II investigation. Phase II will
consist of a more comprehensive study to replicate and build upon the pilot
study results.<o:p></o:p></div>

<div>The research was conducted according to the timeline and major
milestones presented below: The projected duration of the project was 12
months.<o:p></o:p></div>

